{"id": "a9c10655-52dc-46e7-b1bc-4dcd1db1083a", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T11:27:17.345757", "source": "user", "parent_id": "bf8eec4f-56e0-46a9-ac1b-23ef61d2ece1", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"At this stage I would like a focused inspection only. Please do the following, in order, and report back before proposing any full design content. Do not draft the experiment itself yet. I need three things from this first pass: (1) An inspection of the workspace at /workspace, including any existing files, notes, tracker entries, prior runs, or partial designs that may already live there. I would like a short inventory of what is present and a brief excerpt of anything that looks relevant to DrivAerNet++, its 26 parametric descriptors, or the fastback / notchback / estateback variants you plan to address. (2) A proposed approach outline, stated as a set of decision points rather than as a finished plan. In particular, I would like your recommendation on: surrogate model architectures to compare (for example, polynomial regression, Gaussian process, random forest, gradient boosting, and a feed-forward neural network at minimum); the training-set size schedule (the number of distinct sizes and the specific sample counts at each size); resampling protocol at each size to support variance estimation; data partitioning strategy between fastback, notchback, and estateback so that any architecture effect can be disentangled from body-style distribution; the predictive target and its units; the evaluation metrics (for example, R^2, RMSE, MAE, and prediction interval coverage if probabilistic models are included); and the statistical comparison approach for testing whether curves differ across architectures. (3) A short list of consequential assumptions and unresolved uncertainties I should be aware of, including any choice that cannot be made from the brief alone (such as whether the 26 parameters are shared exactly across the three body styles or variant-specific, whether the drag target is coefficient Cd or dimensional force, and how the dataset exposes train/test splits). Keep the reply compact and structured; I will discuss the approach with you before authorising the full design.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"plan\", \"approach_approved\": false, \"public_task\": {\"question\": \"Does the predictive performance of surrogate models based on parametric data improve with increased training set size and differ across model architectures in aerodynamic drag prediction? This experiment uses aerodynamic car designs characterized by 26 parameters—as provided in the DrivAerNet++ dataset, which includes diverse configurations such as fastback, notchback, and estateback—for assessing performance scalability and model-specific behavior.\", \"context\": \"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\", \"environment\": {\"runtime\": \"bubblewrap\", \"network\": \"disabled\", \"repository\": \"/workspace\", \"python\": \"python3\", \"software_environment\": \"system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled\", \"datasets\": [], \"gpu_enabled\": false, \"cpu_affinity_count\": 24, \"note\": \"Successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment.\"}, \"researcher_topics\": [], \"question_budget\": 6, \"actor_step_budget\": 60, \"continue_after_initial_plan\": false}}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 1}
