{"id": "86825706-a527-4b82-955d-e7e714fcbb3e", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T11:27:48.196366", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "c4b05907-21c4-40f9-9aff-9956da224f3f", "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-9bbd5407-edec-49ee-97d5-de844c1d778d", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"phase\": \"exploration\", \"command\": \"grep -i -E \\\"drivaer|fastback|notchback|estateback|surrogate|Cd|aerod\\\" /workspace/task.json; echo \\\"---\\\"; find / -maxdepth 4 -type f \\\\( -iname \\\"*drivaer*\\\" -o -iname \\\"*track.md*\\\" \\\\) 2>/dev/null | head -20; echo \\\"---\\\"; ls -la /workspace/.* 2>/dev/null | head\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.064, \"output\": \"  \\\"question\\\": \\\"Does the predictive performance of surrogate models based on parametric data improve with increased training set size and differ across model architectures in aerodynamic drag prediction? This experiment uses aerodynamic car designs characterized by 26 parameters\\u2014as provided in the DrivAerNet++ dataset, which includes diverse configurations such as fastback, notchback, and estateback\\u2014for assessing performance scalability and model-specific behavior.\\\",\\n---\\n---\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "c4b05907-21c4-40f9-9aff-9956da224f3f", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 11}
{"id": "1ce2a46d-1dca-46d7-89b0-0e201aacd793", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T11:28:19.389996", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "86825706-a527-4b82-955d-e7e714fcbb3e", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-e1fb5cf3-aa3f-4e74-94c3-fc08900a62ab", "tool_call": {"id": "call-e1fb5cf3-aa3f-4e74-94c3-fc08900a62ab", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Inspect workspace, list open decision points and uncertainties\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"## 1. Workspace inventory (`/workspace`)¹\\n\\nOnly one file is present: `task.json`. There are no datasets, no prior runs, no tracker, no partial designs, no notebooks, and no README. The task wrapper includes a `documents.track.md` that states the task is **design-only** and explicitly says repositories and compute are not required. No copy of `track.md` is on disk either. Environmental probe: Python 3.12.3 (system Python; ML libs not preinstalled); network is disabled (`Could not resolve host: www.google.com`); 24 CPU cores available, no GPU.\\n\\nThe only available excerpt about DrivAerNet++ lives inside `task.json` itself:\\n\\n> *\\\"…aerodynamic car designs characterized by 26 parameters—as provided in the DrivAerNet++ dataset, which includes diverse configurations such as fastback, notchback, and estateback…\\\"*\\n\\nKey consequence: **no dataset file, no parameter table, no column names, no target unit, no split policy, and no body-style counts are recoverable from `/workspace`**. Any design that names specific columns, parameter identities, units, or sample counts is making things up. Anything that just talks about N samples, parameter scaling, splits, etc., must explicitly parameterize on these unknowns and surface them as assumptions/inputs to be supplied.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Approach outline — open decision points (not a finished plan)\\n\\n### A. Surrogate architectures to compare (recommended minimum set)\\n\\nI recommend **five** non-trivial families plus a trivial linear baseline:\\n\\n1. **Linear baseline** — ordinary least squares, for sanity / floor.\\n2. **Polynomial regression (degree-2)** — `PolynomialFeatures(degree=2, include_bias=False)` on standardized inputs → ridge regression to keep the 351-feature model identifiable. Captures low-order parametric interactions.\\n3. **Gaussian Process Regression** — anisotropic ARD RBF kernel on standardized X; provides both mean predictions and 95 % prediction intervals (so the probabilistic metric in §F is informative).\\n4. **Random Forest Regressor** — bagged trees, MSE criterion; fixed hyperparameters across sizes for a clean learning curve.\\n5. **Gradient Boosting (HistGradientBoostingRegressor or XGBoost)** — sequential trees; chosen because it is the most common strong baseline on tabular CFD-style data and is known to behave differently from RF as N grows.\\n6. **Feed-forward MLP** — 2 hidden layers (e.g. 128 → 64, ReLU), Adam, early stopping on a fixed internal validation split; deterministic seeding, batch size fixed across sizes.\\n\\nDecision still open: whether to add **Kernel Ridge / SVR (RBF)** as a seventh model, and whether all five should be re-tuned per-size vs. fixed across sizes (see uncertainty 6 below).\\n\\n### B. Training-set size schedule\\n\\nTwo questions:\\n\\n- **Number of distinct sizes.** A learning-curve study typically resolves monotonicity and slope with **5–7 points**. Proposal: **7 points** if N permits, **5 points** otherwise.\\n- **Specific counts.** I cannot fix absolute numbers without N (see §3). Two parameterizable schemes:\\n  - **(Geometric)** `{⌊N_train · 2^k / 2^K⌋ : k = 0..K}` with K = 6 → ≈ 7 points spaced by ×2.\\n  - **(Fixed/illustrative, dependent on N)** something like `{N_min, 2·N_min, 4·N_min, …, N_train}` with N_min chosen so that degree-2 polynomial and MLP are not underdetermined (e.g. N_min ≥ 100–200 for 351 features).\\n- Plus a large held-out **test set** that is **identical across all size points and all replicates**, so architecture comparisons are paired.\\n\\nDecision still open: absolute N_train / N_test and N_min values.\\n\\n### C. Resampling protocol at each size\\n\\n- One fixed seeded test split (stratified by body style; see §D).\\n- For each (architecture, size) cell: **K = 10** random subsamples of the training pool (no replacement) at the target size, with seeded RNG so every architecture sees the **same K training sets**.\\n- Metrics on the fixed test set per replicate → K values per cell → mean = performance, std/sem = variance estimate, distribution = basis for paired tests (see §G).\\n\\nDecision still open: K = 5 vs. K = 10 (budget vs. variance precision).\\n\\n### D. Body-style partitioning (fastback / notchback / estateback)\\n\\nRecommend **two complementary analyses** so architecture effects are not confounded with body-style distribution:\\n\\n- **(i) Pooled, stratified analysis (primary, headline curve).** Train on a stratified random sample across the three body styles (proportional or equal-count — researcher to pick); test on a single stratified held-out set fixed across sizes. This is the curve that directly answers \\\"does performance improve and differ across architectures?\\\".\\n- **(ii) Leave-one-style-out (LOSO) generalization (secondary).** For each body style s ∈ {fastback, notchback, estateback}: train on the *other two* styles only, test on s. Triplets of curves per architecture. Surfaces architectural robustness to body-style shift that the pooled curve can hide.\\n\\nDecision still open: stratified proportional vs. equal-count in (i); whether to also report per-style in-sample curves.\\n\\n### E. Predictive target and units\\n\\nBrief says \\\"aerodynamic drag\\\" only. The design should be **parameterized on the target**: scalar single-target regression, units declared and consistent across all metrics.\\n\\nDecision still open: target ∈ {**drag coefficient Cd** (dimensionless), **drag force F_d** in N at a reference speed/air-density/frontal-area, **pressure drag Cd_p**, or already-normalized target}. Whatever is chosen, all RMSE/MAE will be reported in those units, and any dimensional-normalized metric (e.g. nRMSE by target std) will be reported alongside.\\n\\n### F. Evaluation metrics\\n\\n- **R² (test)**, **RMSE (test)**, **MAE (test)** — all in declared target units.\\n- **Coverage diagnostic** (only when the model is probabilistic): empirical coverage of the 95 % prediction interval and mean interval width on the test set. Applies primarily to GP; suggest reporting for GP and not artificially extending it to trees/NN.\\n- Optional secondary: max absolute error and nRMSE = RMSE / std(y_test).\\n\\n### G. Statistical comparison across architectures\\n\\nTwo complementary layers:\\n\\n- **Per-size test.** Paired test across the K replicates on the **same test set**: paired t-test or Wilcoxon signed-rank, Holm-corrected across the C(C-1)/2 architecture pairs (or against a chosen reference model). Tests vertical separation at each size.\\n- **Curve-level test.** Fit a parametric learning-curve model to each replicate, e.g.\\n  - power law:  RMSE = a + b · n⁻ᶜ,   or   R² = 1 − A · n⁻ᶜ,\\n  - or log-linear on (log n, RMSE / −log(1−R²)).\\n  Then compare per-architecture curve parameters (a or A; exponent c) by paired test across replicates. Tests slope / asymptotic separation and answers *“do learning-curve shapes differ across architectures?”* — directly responsive to the research question.\\n\\nA mixed-effects alternative for the same curve question: linear mixed model with `architecture` and `log(n)` as fixed effects, interaction `architecture × log(n)` tests slope differences, replicate as random intercept.\\n\\nDecision still open: t-test vs. Wilcoxon (assume normality → t; non-robust → Wilcoxon); whether to fit per-architecture curves or just per-size comparisons.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Consequential assumptions and unresolved uncertainties\\n\\nUnresolved / must