 at the target size, with seeded RNG so every architecture sees the **same K training sets**.\n- Metrics on the fixed test set per replicate → K values per cell → mean = performance, std/sem = variance estimate, distribution = basis for paired tests (see §G).\n\nDecision still open: K = 5 vs. K = 10 (budget vs. variance precision).\n\n### D. Body-style partitioning (fastback / notchback / estateback)\n\nRecommend **two complementary analyses** so architecture effects are not confounded with body-style distribution:\n\n- **(i) Pooled, stratified analysis (primary, headline curve).** Train on a stratified random sample across the three body styles (proportional or equal-count — researcher to pick); test on a single stratified held-out set fixed across sizes. This is the curve that directly answers \"does performance improve and differ across architectures?\".\n- **(ii) Leave-one-style-out (LOSO) generalization (secondary).** For each body style s ∈ {fastback, notchback, estateback}: train on the *other two* styles only, test on s. Triplets of curves per architecture. Surfaces architectural robustness to body-style shift that the pooled curve can hide.\n\nDecision still open: stratified proportional vs. equal-count in (i); whether to also report per-style in-sample curves.\n\n### E. Predictive target and units\n\nBrief says \"aerodynamic drag\" only. The design should be **parameterized on the target**: scalar single-target regression, units declared and consistent across all metrics.\n\nDecision still open: target ∈ {**drag coefficient Cd** (dimensionless), **drag force F_d** in N at a reference speed/air-density/frontal-area, **pressure drag Cd_p**, or already-normalized target}. Whatever is chosen, RMSE/MAE will be reported in those units, and any dimensional-normalized metric (e.g. nRMSE by target std) will be reported alongside.\n\n### F. Evaluation metrics\n\n- **R² (test)**, **RMSE (test)**, **MAE (test)** — all in declared target units.\n- **Coverage diagnostic** (only when the model is probabilistic): empirical coverage of the 95 % prediction interval and mean interval width on the test set. Applies primarily to GP; suggest reporting for GP and not artificially extending it to trees/NN.\n- Optional secondary: max absolute error and nRMSE = RMSE / std(y_test).\n\n### G. Statistical comparison across architectures\n\nTwo complementary layers:\n\n- **Per-size test.** Paired test across the K replicates on the **same test set**: paired t-test or Wilcoxon signed-rank, Holm-corrected across the C(C-1)/2 architecture pairs (or against a chosen reference model). Tests vertical separation at each size.\n- **Curve-level test.** Fit a parametric learning-curve model to each replicate, e.g.\n  - power law:  RMSE = a + b * n^(-c),   or   R² = 1 − A * n^(-c),\n  - or log-linear on (log n, RMSE / -log(1−R²)).\n  Then compare per-architecture curve parameters (a or A; exponent c) by paired test across replicates. Tests slope / asymptotic separation and answers *\"do learning-curve shapes differ across architectures?\"* — directly responsive to the research question.\n\nA mixed-effects alternative for the same curve question: linear mixed model with `architecture` and `log(n)` as fixed effects, interaction `architecture × log(n)` tests slope differences, replicate as random intercept.\n\nDecision still open: t-test vs. Wilcoxon (assume normality → t; non-robust → Wilcoxon); whether to fit per-architecture curves or just per-size comparisons.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Consequential assumptions and unresolved uncertainties\n\nUnresolved / must be specified by researcher or by the dataset:\n\n1. **Are the 26 parameters identical across fastback / notchback / estateback, or are some variant-specific** (e.g. DLO/CP height definitions that differ by rear glazing)?\n2. **Target variable**: is it drag coefficient Cd, dimensional drag force F_d, pressure drag Cd_p, or some already-normalized target? At what reference conditions (ρ, U, A)?\n3. **Total sample counts** per style and overall (`N_fastback`, `N_notchback`, `N_estateback`). Without these, the size schedule can only be given as a parameterizable formula.\n4. **Canonical train/test split in DrivAerNet++.** Does the dataset publish one? If not, the experiment must construct and disclose its own (with seed).\n5. **Feature scaling / units / log-scaling conventions** in the published 26 parameters (lengths in mm vs. m, dimensionless ratios already, ride height potentially zero-bounded → log). Needed to specify preprocessing.\n6. **Hyperparameter policy**: fix across training sizes (clean learning curve, simpler) vs. retune per size (more realistic but introduces a tuning-budget confound). Default recommendation: fix across sizes, with a single pre-registered configuration set chosen on a small pilot.\n7. **Whether fastback / notchback / estateback are mutually exclusive or a car design can carry multiple labels** in this dataset.\n8. **Coverage metric scope**: restrict 95 % PI coverage to GP only, or skip entirely if architecture list is finalized as all deterministic.\n9. **Test-set size policy**: identical across all training sizes (paired) vs. size-dependent — recommendation is identical.\n10. **Statistical test multiplicity**: how many pairwise architecture comparisons and per-size tests to Holm-correct over.\n\nOperational notes (not task requirements, but worth flagging): bubblewrap gives 24 CPU cores, no GPU, no network, and ML libraries are not preinstalled. The design will need an explicit assumption about whether the author of the eventual run installs scikit-learn / xgboost / torch from scratch within the budget. The plan being requested is design-only, so this only affects the design's stated feasibility, not what I should do now.\n\n¹ `ls -la /workspace` shows a single file: `task.json` (752 B). No other entries, hidden or otherwise. `find / -maxdepth 4` confirms no DrivAerNet++ file exists anywhere reachable.", "evidence": "Workspace contains only /